	//
	// "Add tool → Connect an MCP server": a tool on a connection that already exists. The server's
	// endpoint and its key are a connection in the Integrations app, made there and never typed
	// here; this names that connection as a tool. Nothing secret is sent.
	//
	// THE WHOLE SERVER, OR THE TOOLS YOU PICK. "One MCP server often exposes several tools — enable
	// the whole server or select tools individually." Leave `mcp_tool_names` out for the whole
	// server, the tools it adds later included; name them to enable those alone.
	//
	// IT STARTS WITH NO AGENT. A new tool's default is "no agents": who may use it is set
	// afterwards, with `PATCH /tools/{tool_id}`.
	//
	// BUILT-IN TOOLS AND ACTIONS ON CONNECTED SYSTEMS ARE NOT ADDED HERE. They are in the catalogue
	// because Onplo or Integrations offers them; only an external MCP server is brought in by hand.
	//
	// IT TAKES TWO PERMISSIONS, as adding a provider profile does: adding a tool, AND pointing
	// something at a connection, `integrations:connection.assign`. Both are asked as one question,
	// and a refusal is one 403 whose detail names both. The connection must be one you may use, of
	// an MCP server; anything else is refused with 422, naming `connection_id`.
	//
	// THE CONNECTION THEN SAYS SO. The Integrations app is told that this tool uses the
	// connection, in the same act, so the connection's "used by" list names it.
	//
	// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
	//
	// Corresponds with POST /tools (the `AddMcpServerTool` operationId).
