// TurnOnAgentWithResponse Turn an agent on
//
// MAKES IT LIVE. "Turn an agent on — make it live so it can work; always a separate
// deliberate step". A draft becomes live, and so does a paused agent: the page says a paused
// one "runs again only once someone who stays turns it on", so bringing it back is this act
// and this permission, never the one that paused it.
//
// ALREADY LIVE IS NOT AN ERROR. The agent is answered as it stands.
//
// WHAT IS REFUSED (409). An agent that has stopped for good — one a member built for
// themselves, after that member left the company — "never runs here again". And one whose
// change is waiting for approval is turned on by that approval, not by this.
//
// THE TWO THINGS THE PAGE PUTS IN FRONT OF TURNING ON ARE NOT BUILT: the agent's tests, and
// the proposal gate. Both are a later package's, so in this slice nothing blocks it.
//
// A LIVE AGENT DOES NO WORK IN THIS SLICE. Nothing can start one yet.
//
// WHO TURNED IT ON LENDS NOTHING, AND IS IN THE AUDIT RECORD OF THE ACT ALONE (decision
// 0879). An agent working for a person borrows that person's reach; in a business environment
// an agent that runs fully on its own has a role of its own, set on its page; in a personal
// environment an agent always borrows its owner's. None of the three is the reach of whoever
// turned it on, so it is on no field of the agent.
//
// TURNING ON IS DEPLOYING, a build act of its own: `ai:agent.deploy` (ONPLO-3161, security
// baseline §3.20). A person may, and so may a key acting as one. A machine's key may only
// behind a build door an owner opened, and while nothing keeps that door it is refused 403
// `ring_requires_person` (decision 0803). An agent asks: it may propose turning one on, and a
// person presses the button, as the catalogue marks every deploy. Until ONPLO-3164 this asked
// `ai:agent.run`, which let any credential holding it turn one on.
//
