// ListChatsWithResponse The chats you may see
//
// "HISTORY LISTS THE CHAT CARRIED ON MOST RECENTLY FIRST." One row per chat: its name, the agent
// it keeps, and its two moments, when it was started and when it was last carried on.
//
// ONLY WHAT YOU MAY SEE, asked of each row and never at the door (decision 0693). One you hold
// nothing on is not in this list — absent, never masked.
//
// YOUR OWN, AND THE ONES SHARED WITH YOU. "Share a single chat with someone → it appears in
// their Shared with me", and in this app that is the list the chats already sit in. A chat
// somebody shared with you is a row here among your own, in the same order, for as long as the
// share stands; `owner_person_id` says whose it is. A share that ended, and a chat its owner
// sent to Trash, are no longer rows. Holding the permission across the whole account
// environment lists nobody's chat: "There is no company-wide chat feed."
//
// TRASH IS ITS OWN LIST. `in_trash=true` lists your own chats in Trash: "nobody it was shared
// with reaches it there". Without it, no chat in Trash is listed.
//
// NARROWED TO WHAT WAS SHARED WITH YOU: `shared=with_me`. "Shared with me" and "Shared with
// others" are, in this app, "FILTERS on the list the objects already sit in" (the shared
// rules). With it the list holds the chats somebody shared with you and none of your own, in
// the same order, each still asked of identity. With `in_trash=true` beside it the list is
// empty: nothing shared with you is in your Trash. A cursor continues under the filter it
// was handed out under, or without one; a cursor that names one of your own chats is refused
// under the filter, `cursor_invalid`.
//
// NARROWED TO WHAT YOU SHARED: `shared=with_others`. "“Shared with others” shows the chats you
// shared yourself. Narrowed to it, the list holds every chat on which you placed a share that
// still lives. A chat of yours that only its Manager shared onward is in the Manager's list
// and not in yours." Which shares you placed is the identity service's to say, and this list
// asks it. So a chat somebody else owns is a row here when you placed a share on it as its
// Manager and still reach it; one of yours that only its Manager shared is not. In the same
// order, each still asked of identity. With `in_trash=true` beside it the list holds your own
// chats in Trash that carry a share you placed and identity still lists: nobody reaches them
// there, and bringing one back brings its shares back. A cursor continues under the filter it
// was handed out under, or without one; a cursor that names a chat you placed no share on is
// refused under the filter, `cursor_invalid`. This said "THE OTHER DIRECTION IS NOT DECLARED"
// until 14 October 2026.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /chats (the `ListChats` operationId).
