// AddPersonalProviderKeyWithBodyWithResponse Bring your own key into a company
//
// CAN SOMEBODY USE THEIR OWN API KEY INSIDE A COMPANY? Yes — if they hold the permission for
// it, and that is the whole control. A company that does not want a person's private provider
// account touching its data does not grant it.
//
// It answers a provider profile like any other, with `owner_scope: personal` and you as its
// owner. The connection it names must be one of your own PERSONAL connections in this
// environment; anything else is refused with 422, naming `connection_id`.
//
// ALLOWED, VISIBLE AND TEMPORARY. The profile is marked personal wherever it appears, so the
// company sees what it depends on and does not control. When you leave, your connections are
// deleted and never inherited, and this profile goes with them: whatever ran on it stops and
// says so where it broke.
//
// ITS OWN OPERATION, because it is its own permission, and an operation carries one
// permission of its own. A BUSINESS ENVIRONMENT ONLY: in a personal environment every
// profile is already your own, the permission does not exist there, and
// `POST /provider-profiles` is the way.
//
// IT TAKES A SECOND PERMISSION TOO, as adding any profile does: pointing something at a
// connection, `integrations:connection.assign`, on your own personal connection. Both are
// asked as one question, and a refusal is one 403 whose detail names both.
//
// YOUR CONNECTION THEN SAYS SO, as for any profile: its "used by" list names this one.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /personal-provider-keys (the `AddPersonalProviderKey` operationId).
