// PickProviderProfileConnectionWithBodyWithResponse Pick a new key for a profile whose connection was removed
//
// "⚠ CONNECTION REMOVED — PICK A NEW ONE." When a company profile's connection is removed for
// good in the Integrations app, the profile stays, with `connection_removed_at` set, and every
// agent keeps pointing at it (decision 0723). This is how it gets its key back: it now points
// at the connection you name, and `connection_removed_at` is `null` again. Its name, its id
// and everything that points at it are unchanged.
//
// ONLY WHILE ITS CONNECTION IS REMOVED. A profile whose connection still exists is one key's
// row, and pointing it elsewhere would be another profile under an old name: that is refused
// with 422, naming `connection_id` — remove it and add one. A personal profile never reaches
// this: it goes with its connection.
//
// THE CONNECTION IS JUDGED AS ADDING A PROFILE JUDGES IT. It must be a company connection in
// this account environment that you may use, of a system that is an AI provider. One that does
// not exist for you, one you may not use, one of another kind and a personal one are all
// refused the same way: 422, naming `connection_id`.
//
// IT TAKES TWO PERMISSIONS, THE PAIR ADDING ONE TAKES: changing the profile, and pointing
// something at a connection, which is a permission of the Integrations app:
// `integrations:connection.assign`. Your credential must carry both. They are asked as one
// question, and a refusal is one 403 whose detail names both.
//
// THE CONNECTION THEN SAYS SO. The Integrations app is told that this profile uses the new
// connection, in the same act, so its "used by" list names the profile. The removed one took
// its list with it.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /provider-profiles/{provider_profile_id}/connection (the `PickProviderProfileConnection` operationId).
